# Map: Multi-dev maintenance model for the generated docs directory

## Destination

A locked maintenance model for the shared generated-docs directory (rules, wiki,
machine-appended conventions log) under ~10 concurrent devs: scan ownership,
shared-vs-per-clone state, and merge behavior for machine-appended files decided,
ready to slice into tooling changes. Rewriting the doc-generation pipeline itself
is explicitly out of scope.

## Notes

- Hard constraint: automation and hooks never commit. Anything shared through git
  must be written by a deliberate, human-gated step.
- Team: ~10 devs, trunk-based, short feature branches, mixed merge-based and
  rebase pulls. A couple of teammates never installed the repo's git hooks —
  every candidate design must survive hook-less clones.
- The pipeline was designed for a single developer; decisions here retrofit it
  for concurrency, they do not redesign it.

## Decisions so far

<!-- the index — one line per closed ticket; the detail lives in the ticket -->

## Not yet specified

- Deterministic content-anchoring of docs to the source they describe (hash-based
  drift detection instead of LLM re-reads) — suspected necessary for silent drift,
  but not phrasable sharply until scan ownership and shared-vs-per-clone state land.
- Long-horizon doc lifecycle hygiene — superseding rather than deleting rules,
  freshness metadata, retiring doc-demand once coverage lands — wanted, but nobody
  can yet say what it needs.
- Review-gate / rule-ownership design for shared-doc changes — cannot be judged
  until the branch-protection prerequisite ticket returns platform facts.

## Out of scope

- Rewriting the doc-generation pipeline itself — destination is the maintenance
  model around it, per the destination grill.

